# Highwayman

**Alignments.** Typically anarchist or evil. Robin Hood types, and those who
victimise only the Coalition, other bandits or evil forces, may be any
alignment including good.

The Highwayman is a specialist in bushwhacking, ambush and robbery on the road
and in the wilderness. Some ride alone; others lead a small band of four to
twelve outlaws - bandits, thieves, other Highwaymen, sorcerers, Psi-Stalkers,
Simvan.

Most live by the philosophy that only the strong should survive and that the
weak, foolish and careless are fair game. The credos the book gives them all
run the same way: the weak exist to be used, might makes right, and possession
settles ownership. Most are tough opportunists who will stoop to any level to
further their own ends.

## Method

What separates a Highwayman from the run-of-the-mill bandit is daring, risk,
horsemanship and ruthlessness. They are tricky - setting traps and ambushes for
travellers, and making dramatic entrances calculated to frighten and
intimidate. They exploit surprise, carelessness and complacency constantly, and
are masters at recognising an opportunity large or small.

To manufacture surprise they will cause diversions with explosives, fire,
falling trees or magic; plant an inside man among the apparently innocent
riders on a stagecoach or the victims of a bank robbery; and use disguise and
pure daring. Some have military backgrounds, some are extremely well educated,
and others have simply always been armed robbers.

They are amazing horsemen and hovercycle pilots, which is what the trade is
built on.

## Lore

Also known as the Bushwhacker, the Highway Robber and the Highjacker. The book
presents the Highwayman as an NPC villain first and a player character second,
and says outright that evil ones make the better villains.
